WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:04.799
Photogrammetry is quite straightforward using a smartphone.

2
00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:08.039
There are several apps that make the process painless.

3
00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:12.480
They mostly work the same way with slight variations and limitations.

4
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.120
The idea is to take pictures from different angles around the subject,

5
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.420
and then you process the model onto the cloud before downloading it ready to use.

6
00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:25.699
Some applications won't let you use a turntable,

7
00:00:25.699 --> 00:00:28.500
Epic Games' Reality Scan being one of them.

8
00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:34.109
Many free apps won't let you export a model until you pay for the full version.

9
00:00:34.109 --> 00:00:36.950
The good news is that Reality Scan is free,

10
00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:43.679
but you will need to export your model to Sketchfab first prior to downloading it to a PC.

11
00:00:43.679 --> 00:00:50.640
Other apps, like the free version of Curie Engine, restricts you to only 70 input pictures.

12
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.880
The workflow is usually painless.

13
00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:56.759
Here's a quick example of how Curie Engine operates.

14
00:00:56.759 --> 00:00:59.560
I'm using Curie Engine as an example,

15
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.320
because I'm using a turntable as part of my setup,

16
00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.200
and Reality Scan won't let me use that.

17
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:09.599
With Reality Scan, the model is static and you need to move around it.

18
00:01:09.599 --> 00:01:13.239
At any rate, the process is quite similar between apps.

19
00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:29.439
You start the app, create a new project, take pictures,

20
00:01:29.439 --> 00:01:31.359
try not to change your zoom levels,

21
00:01:31.359 --> 00:02:09.639
but you are encouraged to move in and out to get extra detail.

22
00:02:09.639 --> 00:02:21.490
You then proceed to fill the project data before you send it to the cloud.

23
00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:22.729
Then you wait.

24
00:02:22.729 --> 00:02:30.240
Processing may take a while based on network traffic.

25
00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.919
Alternately, the model is created,

26
00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:45.889
and you can preview it before exporting it to your email account.

27
00:02:45.889 --> 00:02:48.330
Although I'm using a turntable in this example,

28
00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:53.300
I will show you one advantage that Reality Scan has over Curie Engine.

29
00:02:53.300 --> 00:03:00.409
When you start a project using Reality Scan,

30
00:03:00.409 --> 00:03:04.169
you can choose to use or disable Auto Capture mode.

31
00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:08.979
I will disable it in this example to have better control of taking pictures,

32
00:03:08.979 --> 00:03:11.580
but you can opt to leave it on.

33
00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:14.139
As you start taking pictures in Reality Scan,

34
00:03:14.139 --> 00:03:17.259
remember again that you must turn around the subject.

35
00:03:17.259 --> 00:03:22.219
Therefore, you need to move the camera around for the app to perform well.

36
00:03:22.219 --> 00:03:26.099
What is different here is that as you take pictures,

37
00:03:26.099 --> 00:03:30.500
the app creates a ghost snapshot of the previous photos.

38
00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:34.689
That makes it easy to see how the photo shoot is proceeding.

39
00:03:34.729 --> 00:03:42.360
Thus, identifying gaps where you need more pictures taken.

40
00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:46.840
I will cancel this project as I cannot go fully around the model using this setup,

41
00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:49.300
but you get the gist.

42
00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:53.740
At any rate, scanning 3D models using a photogrammetry app

43
00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:58.300
means you often need to clean it up before you get it into Unreal Engine.

44
00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:01.620
And so, once you have downloaded it to your PC,

45
00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:14.430
you may want to process it in a DCC application to remove any unwanted data.

46
00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:18.189
In this case, note that not only Leo was scanned,

47
00:04:18.189 --> 00:04:31.230
but also the turntable and a bit of the desktop as well.

48
00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:34.350
So you can use your DCC tools to clean that up

49
00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:51.629
and leave only the part that interests you.

50
00:04:51.629 --> 00:04:56.040
Once you have removed the excess, you can analyze your model.

51
00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:58.399
You'll notice that it generally looks quite good

52
00:04:58.399 --> 00:05:01.779
for the minimal effort you put creating it.

53
00:05:01.779 --> 00:05:04.220
There are some areas that look weaker,

54
00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:08.839
such as the underbelly that didn't have much data to work with.

55
00:05:08.879 --> 00:05:12.019
There weren't many photos taken from that angle.

56
00:05:12.019 --> 00:05:14.379
So, although it's not perfect,

57
00:05:14.379 --> 00:05:24.329
it's still remarkably good for the ease in which it was generated.

58
00:05:24.329 --> 00:05:28.529
From there on, you can export the FBX file over writing the old one

59
00:05:28.529 --> 00:05:47.829
or simply creating a new one

60
00:05:47.829 --> 00:06:31.459
and import it into Unreal Engine as a final model.

61
00:06:31.459 --> 00:06:34.620
Of course, you can always fine-tune things

62
00:06:34.620 --> 00:07:05.750
such as adjusting specular and roughness properties for better results.

63
00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:09.389
In the next video, we look at RealityCapture,

64
00:07:09.389 --> 00:07:13.790
which gives you a wealth of features to output models with a much higher quality.
